#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"}] :question/id #uuid "13cf1317-c3a1-5175-9f5b-c3da13cad348" :question/math-academy-id "e-6744" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (\\lambda-4)^6(\\lambda-9)^8,$ the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ and the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14 \\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 14-9 = 5.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ of dimension $5$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 = 14-7 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ of dimension $7$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $5$ in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 7-5=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/id #uuid "faa37d23-bdd2-5651-bf53-47835ee61cc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9055" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5,$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1,$ there are exactly $3$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5,\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda = 1$ has multiplicity $5.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $5.$\n\nHowever, there are only $3$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 1$ is $5-3=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/id #uuid "bed5f45f-5c76-542b-965f-eadf474823c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9125" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (3+\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-\\pi)^8(\\lambda-2)^3,$ the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^3$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^4$ equals $6.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ has dimension $6.$ This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $6.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^3 = 6-4=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=\\pi.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195c4aec-4206-524d-999f-7c4101d2d7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386447} {:answer/id #uuid "ba293136-d659-561c-8537-09a5722e5b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386448} {:answer/id #uuid "96a612d3-8f1e-5786-ae51-b97758955c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386449} {:answer/id #uuid "52d296bc-bcdf-549e-baff-a4abe5bc23b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386450} {:answer/id #uuid "61959f7f-7993-5031-83b4-69a5c25709c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a612d3-8f1e-5786-ae51-b97758955c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386449} :answer-field/id #uuid "480350c9-2945-54c3-8987-b479d87ce01c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdcb3137-9f9a-53d4-9695-935580d7ee98" :question/math-academy-id "q-109666" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -3)^{2}(\\lambda +2)^{4},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -3)^{2}(\\lambda +2)^{4},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $3-2=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6bab197-73af-5629-bd73-9a2539823707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173741} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1ab9c9-85f4-531d-b3a7-bea26e2d3c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173742} {:answer/id #uuid "3c42c8d8-a86c-5a0e-9310-317060bb58bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173743} {:answer/id #uuid "5029f1b5-3ff3-57ee-bad8-a1511f8c44c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173744} {:answer/id #uuid "69acfa87-eb10-5b78-aa51-34879b469042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173745}] :answer-field/id #uuid "575ab359-7284-535c-aa54-794bcba6804d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173740}] :question/id #uuid "3cb467c0-ea77-596f-b372-4352d634576f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109740" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(λ) = λ^{3}(4 - λ)^{2}(7 - λ)^{6}$, and for the eigenvalue $λ = 0$, there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $R^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $λ = 0$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "382f6341-07e9-5d75-adb4-5a4fcdfe016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386454} {:answer/id #uuid "a98c0a47-fad4-54bd-9a08-1016c8c5e268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386455} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e5851d-1f19-524f-9845-a03add07f035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386456} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4a0c0b-1762-5e1b-a78b-303307949962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386457} {:answer/id #uuid "235fcb2d-aad6-533c-8d07-75611f87ebf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "382f6341-07e9-5d75-adb4-5a4fcdfe016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386454} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4f5269a-bb75-592f-9f58-18875884cd1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a37675f-e872-5fca-bf67-03df4f2e5cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-109741" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $S$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(e-\\lambda )^{5}(\\lambda +\\sqrt{3})^{4}(5-\\lambda )^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{16}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $S,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(e-\\lambda )^{5}(\\lambda +\\sqrt{3})^{4}(5-\\lambda )^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =e$ has multiplicity $5.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e$ is $5.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =e$ is $5-2=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71f7292-bd10-52a5-b0a7-f6ea545eff02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375722} {:answer/id #uuid "0925206f-5716-5628-8e80-af76824fd5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375723} {:answer/id #uuid "978a317f-5603-5b4d-b70c-bb75018431e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375724} {:answer/id #uuid "073891a0-1fd5-5d8b-9990-d4208d56e2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375725} {:answer/id #uuid "543cbc6b-d49e-5c0e-83cb-83010e06d3f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "978a317f-5603-5b4d-b70c-bb75018431e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375724} :answer-field/id #uuid "3538ec67-039b-5162-a8f1-3e30a944e77d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fab609bc-c26d-54d9-ae3c-82ed6883e1ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-109840" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $S$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(7+\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda -\\sqrt{3})^{4}(\\lambda +2)^{3},$ the nullity of $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{10}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $S,$ how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $S$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ca8c085-7c51-5fff-9961-15766ba7c2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173765} {:answer/id #uuid "e866e7d2-9b40-51b2-9dee-3784a86a46d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173766} {:answer/id #uuid "25d039c9-fc79-5e98-add2-8b994077fb15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173767} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec676c9-10cb-5606-a067-04430e7fb666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173768} {:answer/id #uuid "46cb3f63-e3c3-570d-bba9-771618b85b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173769}] :answer-field/id #uuid "69155cb0-b914-560f-b4ff-99ca1bdd25de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173764} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f99e9009-e6c2-582d-90ae-d0bfcf1f5d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362725} {:answer/id #uuid "99d135b5-1334-5d73-b629-c04ae8d41c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362726} {:answer/id #uuid "2dac6776-db44-5e53-8d0e-7467411d2060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362727} {:answer/id #uuid "ac89cd1b-b62b-50dd-b0a5-0452ce5ed0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362728} {:answer/id #uuid "34ffef47-4e58-50ef-b079-896389f7f959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f99e9009-e6c2-582d-90ae-d0bfcf1f5d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362725} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd562744-db8f-593a-9c8b-77f2c75d981e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27ab5139-4b6b-5639-b90d-136b8a14f7bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109843" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}(\\lambda +1)^{16}(\\lambda -8)^{2},$ the nullity of $(A+I)^{4}$ equals $4,$ and the nullity of $(A+I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A+I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A+I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$ has dimension $7.$ This means that the solutions to $(A+I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $7.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{4}=7-4=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94209dd4-8332-5a59-a665-1704486cfa93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175553} {:answer/id #uuid "a5527931-a3b1-55fa-9b63-7717e8e64f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175554} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa7a214-ba76-554e-beac-ba26ce0b267b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175555} {:answer/id #uuid "556528e8-e14c-5170-a694-d9d52bcbb8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175556} {:answer/id #uuid "7cda7705-15f5-55b4-9121-da930449a99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175557}] :answer-field/id #uuid "24a6c438-eab2-5a25-b355-d190884b3e3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175552}] :question/id #uuid "e04a0ca0-a400-5c37-86e4-06c47bca3608" :question/math-academy-id "q-78160" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(λ) = λ(λ + 1)^{3}(λ - 2)^{4}$, the rank of $(A - 2I)^{2}$ equals $7$, and the rank of $(A - 2I)^{3}$ equals $6$. If a basis of $R^{8}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e865e05-d063-5259-9427-8e9ac3415420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d54eb7-8c94-5ba1-8ff1-0e33e8e8ff4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364423} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca18b90-c676-50ef-bb00-24c4e41c4b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364424} {:answer/id #uuid "61161b93-ea6b-5325-aa7b-d6e1119b0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364425} {:answer/id #uuid "1c60cb57-ff4f-5029-8ce2-b8fe29a39c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e865e05-d063-5259-9427-8e9ac3415420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b1e17b-e37b-5c86-92c1-41321a65eb42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "246e62ce-be4d-58bc-8aa3-d8a9e5b8b661" :question/math-academy-id "q-78463" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(1+\\lambda )^{4}(1-\\lambda )^{2}(4-\\lambda )^{3},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1$ is $4-1=3.$"}]}
